<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Ligurian Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1805 CE · capital Genoa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French-sponsored republic that replaced the old Republic of Genoa in 1797 and was annexed to Napoleon's empire in 1805.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In May 1797 a democratic rising in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, overthrew the old aristocratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-mombello">Convention of Mombello</a> in June 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> reorganised it as the Ligurian Republic, with a constitution on the French model.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The new republic abolished noble privilege, but the French controlled its policy and took its resources. In 1800 the Austrians and British besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, held by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">Masséna</a>, causing terrible famine before the city surrendered shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Marengo</a> restored French dominance. A new constitution of 1802 restored the title of doge, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-durazzo">Girolamo Durazzo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In June 1805 the Ligurian Republic was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> fall the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa</a> (1814) was briefly restored before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ligurian-republic.jpg" alt="Ligurian Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Genoa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1805 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Genoa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Girolamo Luigi Durazzo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ligurian, Italian, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Ligure</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1801&amp;focus=ligurian-republic" aria-label="Ligurian Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4894 607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ligurian Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5214 863L5214 863M5214 863L5201 869M5214 863L5054 728M5214 863L5054 728M5214 863L5214 863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5214 863h0M5201 869h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Genoa. <a href="/atlas?year=1801&amp;focus=ligurian-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
